\title{Explicit Graceful Labelings of Paths and Stars,\\with an Edge-Partition Counting Principle}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{29 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A graceful labeling of a graph with $m$ edges is an injective assignment of vertex labels from $\{0,1,\ldots,m\}$ such that the absolute differences across edges are exactly $\{1,2,\ldots,m\}$. The Graceful Tree Conjecture asserts that every finite tree admits such a labeling and remains open in full generality. This paper develops two explicit infinite families and the elementary counting principle underlying their connection to graph decompositions. Every path with $n$ edges is gracefully labeled by the alternating sequence $0,n,1,n-1,2,n-2,\ldots$; a closed formula is given, injectivity is proved by parity analysis, and consecutive edge differences are shown to be $n,n-1,\ldots,1$. Every star with $n$ leaves is gracefully labeled by assigning $0$ to the center and $1,\ldots,n$ to the leaves. Finally, if the edge set of a finite host graph is partitioned into $r$ pieces of $m$ edges each, then the host has $rm$ edges. This gives the necessary divisibility condition for complete-graph decompositions into equal tree copies. The constructions yield linear-time labeling algorithms and transparent certificates, while identifying precise next problems concerning caterpillars, olive trees, leaf extensions, and cyclic decompositions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph. A vertex labeling turns each edge $\{u,v\}$ into a numerical difference $|f(u)-f(v)|$. A graceful labeling asks for the most economical possible arrangement: if $G$ has $m$ edges, the vertices receive distinct labels from $0$ through $m$, and the edge differences exhaust every positive integer from $1$ through $m$.

The definition creates a rigid interaction between local and global data. Locally, every adjacent pair must have a nonzero difference in the permitted interval. Globally, all $m$ target differences must occur. When $G$ is a tree with $m$ edges, it has $m+1$ vertices. Consequently, injectivity into the $m+1$ labels $0,1,\ldots,m$ forces every label to be used. A graceful tree therefore realizes two simultaneous bijections: its vertices occupy the whole label interval, and its edges occupy the whole difference interval.

The Graceful Tree Conjecture states that every finite tree is graceful. Although its formulation is elementary, the conjecture remains unresolved. It is therefore important to distinguish the universal claim from explicit families for which complete constructions are available. This paper proves gracefulness for two fundamental families: paths and stars. These families represent opposite degree profiles. A path distributes degree along a linear spine, whereas a star concentrates all edges at one center. Their successful labelings accordingly use different mechanisms: alternating extremes for paths and direct radial differences for stars.

Graceful labelings are also naturally associated with decomposition questions. A decomposition of a host graph partitions its edge set into copies of a smaller graph. Before any structural construction can succeed, its edge counts must agree. We prove the general counting identity for a finite exact edge partition and specialize it to complete graphs. This result separates the arithmetic requirement from the harder tasks of proving disjointness and coverage.

The contributions are: a closed and proved formula for the alternating path labeling; a proof that its consecutive differences are $n,n-1,\ldots,1$; a proof that the center-and-leaves labeling gracefully labels every star; an exact cardinality theorem for finite edge partitions into equally sized pieces; and linear-time construction and verification algorithms. No claim is made here that all trees, all caterpillars, or all olive trees are graceful. The scope is precisely paths, stars, and the counting law for exact edge partitions.

\section{Definitions and basic consequences}

\subsection{Finite simple graphs and trees}

A \emph{finite simple graph} is a pair $G=(V,E)$ in which $V$ is finite and $E$ is a set of two-element subsets of $V$. Edges are undirected, loops are excluded, and no pair supports more than one edge. The number of edges is $|E|$.

The path with $n$ edges, denoted $P_{n+1}$, has vertex set $\{0,1,\ldots,n\}$ and edge set
\[
 \bigl\{\{i,i+1\}:0\le i<n\bigr\}.
\]
The star with $n$ leaves, denoted $S_n$, consists of a center $c$, leaves $\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_n$, and precisely the edges
\[
 \bigl\{\{c,\ell_j\}:1\le j\le n\bigr\}.
\]
Both are trees. The case $n=0$ is allowed and gives one isolated vertex.

\begin{definition}[Graceful labeling]
Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph and let $m$ be a nonnegative integer. A function
\[
 f:V\longrightarrow\{0,1,\ldots,m\}
\]
is a \emph{graceful labeling with parameter $m$} if it is injective, every edge difference lies in $\{1,\ldots,m\}$, and for every $d\in\{1,\ldots,m\}$ some edge $\{u,v\}\in E$ satisfies $|f(u)-f(v)|=d$. When $m=|E|$, this is the standard notion. A graph is \emph{graceful} if such a labeling exists.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Uniqueness of realized differences]
Suppose $G$ has exactly $m$ edges and a labeling realizes every difference in $\{1,\ldots,m\}$. Then each difference occurs on exactly one edge.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
There are $m$ edges and $m$ required values. A surjection from the edge set to the difference set is therefore a bijection. If one value occurred twice, another would be absent.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Full use of labels for trees]
If a tree with $m$ edges is gracefully labeled from $\{0,1,\ldots,m\}$, every number in the interval labels exactly one vertex.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A finite tree with $m$ edges has $m+1$ vertices. An injection between two sets of size $m+1$ is a bijection.
\end{proof}

\section{The alternating path construction}

Fix $n\ge0$. For $i\in\{0,1,\ldots,n\}$ define
\[
L_n(i)=
\begin{cases}
 i/2,&\text{if $i$ is even},\\[4pt]
 n-\lfloor i/2\rfloor,&\text{if $i$ is odd}.
\end{cases}
\]
Thus the labels in path order are $0,n,1,n-1,2,n-2,\ldots$. The construction interleaves an increasing low sequence with a decreasing high sequence.

\begin{lemma}[Range bound]
For every $i\in\{0,1,\ldots,n\}$, $0\le L_n(i)\le n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $i$ is even, $L_n(i)=i/2$ is nonnegative and at most $i\le n$. If $i$ is odd, $\lfloor i/2\rfloor\le n$, so $n-\lfloor i/2\rfloor$ lies between $0$ and $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Injectivity]
The function $L_n$ is injective on $\{0,1,\ldots,n\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write even positions as $2a$ and odd positions as $2b+1$. At even positions $L_n(2a)=a$, so equal labels imply equal positions. At odd positions $L_n(2b+1)=n-b$, with the same conclusion. A collision between an even and odd position would give $a=n-b$, hence $a+b=n$. But $2a\le n$ and $2b+1\le n$ imply $2(a+b)+1\le2n$, whereas $a+b=n$ implies $2n+1\le2n$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Consecutive-difference formula]
For every integer $i$ with $0\le i<n$,
\[
 |L_n(i+1)-L_n(i)|=n-i.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $i=2a$, then $L_n(i)=a$ and $L_n(i+1)=n-a$. Hence the difference is $n-2a=n-i$. If $i=2a+1$, then $L_n(i)=n-a$ and $L_n(i+1)=a+1$, so the difference is $n-2a-1=n-i$. The inequalities follow from $i<n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Graceful Path Theorem]
For every nonnegative integer $n$, the path $P_{n+1}$ with $n$ edges is graceful. The labeling $L_n$ is injective, takes values in $\{0,1,\ldots,n\}$, and its edge differences are exactly $\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The range and injectivity follow from the preceding lemmas. The edges are precisely $\{i,i+1\}$ for $0\le i<n$. Their differences are $n-i$, which run through $n,n-1,\ldots,1$ exactly once.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $n=8$, the labels are
\[
 0,8,1,7,2,6,3,5,4,
\]
and the edge differences are $8,7,6,5,4,3,2,1$. For $n=0$, the one vertex receives label $0$ and the required difference set is empty.
\end{example}

\section{The center-and-leaves star construction}

Fix $n\ge0$. Define $R_n(c)=0$ and $R_n(\ell_j)=j$ for $1\le j\le n$.

\begin{lemma}[Range and injectivity for stars]
The labels $R_n(c),R_n(\ell_1),\ldots,R_n(\ell_n)$ are distinct and all lie in $\{0,1,\ldots,n\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The center has label $0$, while the leaves have the pairwise distinct positive labels $1$ through $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Radial difference formula]
For each $j\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$, the edge $\{c,\ell_j\}$ has difference $j$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
One has $|R_n(\ell_j)-R_n(c)|=|j-0|=j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Graceful Star Theorem]
Every star $S_n$ is graceful. The center-and-leaves labeling realizes the edge differences $1,2,\ldots,n$ exactly once.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first star lemma gives a valid injective labeling. Every edge is incident to the center, and the radial difference formula gives difference $j$ on the edge ending at $\ell_j$. Thus all and only the required differences occur.
\end{proof}

The path and star constructions expose complementary principles. On a path, adjacency is controlled by alternating low and high labels. On a star, all differences are controlled by anchoring the center at the extreme label $0$. More elaborate families must coordinate these mechanisms across branching structure.

\section{Exact edge partitions and counting}

Let $K=(W,F)$ be a finite simple host graph, let $I$ be a finite index set, and for each $a\in I$ let $F_a$ be a finite set of edges of $K$.

\begin{definition}[Exact edge partition]
The family $(F_a)_{a\in I}$ is an \emph{exact edge partition} of $K$ if $F_a\subseteq F$ for every $a$, and every edge $e\in F$ belongs to exactly one $F_a$.
\end{definition}

The second condition combines coverage with uniqueness. Equivalently,
\[
 F=\bigcup_{a\in I}F_a
\]
and the sets $F_a$ are pairwise disjoint.

\begin{theorem}[Edge-Partition Counting Theorem]
Suppose $(F_a)_{a\in I}$ is an exact edge partition of a finite graph $K$. If every piece has exactly $m$ edges, then
\[
 |F|=|I|m.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Exact coverage gives $F=\bigcup_{a\in I}F_a$. Exact uniqueness makes the pieces pairwise disjoint: an edge in both $F_a$ and $F_b$ would have two indices unless $a=b$. Cardinality is additive over a finite disjoint union, so
\[
 |F|=\sum_{a\in I}|F_a|=\sum_{a\in I}m=|I|m.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Divisibility condition]
If a finite graph is decomposed into copies of a graph having $m$ edges, then $m$ divides the number of host edges.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
If there are $r$ copies, the theorem gives $|F|=rm$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Complete-graph condition]
If the complete graph on $q$ vertices is decomposed into copies of an $m$-edge graph, then
\[
 m\mid\binom q2=\frac{q(q-1)}2.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A complete graph has one edge for each unordered pair of distinct vertices, hence $\binom q2$ edges. Apply the divisibility corollary.
\end{proof}

These are necessary conditions, not sufficient ones. Correct cardinality does not prevent pieces from overlapping or guarantee complete coverage. A decomposition proof must separately establish containment, disjointness, and coverage.

A graceful labeling of an $m$-edge tree distinguishes its edges by lengths $1,\ldots,m$. This spectrum suggests translation constructions in cyclic groups: place labels in a cyclic set, translate all labels by a common offset, and study the copies. If the translated edge sets are pairwise disjoint and cover the complete host, they form an exact partition. The counting theorem then confirms the edge count, but does not itself establish the construction.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Alternating path labeling}

Given $n$, evaluate $L_n(i)$ for $i=0,\ldots,n$ using the parity formula. This takes $O(n)$ time and stores $O(n)$ output labels; streamed generation uses only $O(1)$ auxiliary space. A certificate consists of labels and consecutive differences. With a Boolean presence array or hash set, one checks range, distinctness, and the target difference set in expected $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ space. Sorting instead yields $O(n\log n)$ time.

\subsection{Radial star labeling}

Given $n$, output center label $0$ and leaf labels $1,\ldots,n$. Explicit output takes $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ space. Formula-level random access to any label or edge difference takes $O(1)$ time.

\subsection{Exact partition audit}

For a host edge set and proposed pieces, maintain a frequency map from edges to occurrence counts. Reject non-host edges, increment each listed edge, and accept exactly when every host edge has frequency one and every piece has the prescribed size. If $M$ edge occurrences are supplied, expected running time is $O(M+|F|)$ and space is $O(|F|)$. This checks the hypotheses of the counting theorem.

\section{Applications and computational use}

The explicit constructions are useful benchmarks for graceful-labeling searches. Paths test parity and adjacency order; stars test degree concentration and extreme labels. Their closed-form spectra make errors easy to identify.

In network design, an edge difference may represent a separation class, channel offset, time lag, or resource distance. A graceful assignment guarantees that every class from $1$ through $m$ appears once. The interpretation varies, but the combinatorial guarantee does not.

The partition theorem supports decomposition experiments. Divisibility is an inexpensive filter for impossible parameters. A frequency audit then checks coverage for candidates that pass. Graceful labelings provide structured templates rather than arbitrary pieces and can exploit host symmetry.

Finite computation cannot prove the universal Graceful Tree Conjecture. It can refute a candidate formula, discover patterns, and validate bounded instances. A failed formula exhibits repeated labels, an out-of-range value, or a repeated or missing edge difference. A failed decomposition exhibits overlap or an uncovered edge.

\section{Discussion}

The path and star theorems prove gracefulness at two extremes of tree geometry. The path has maximum degree two and a canonical linear order. The star concentrates all degree at one center. For paths, parity determines whether a label comes from the low or high end, and step $i$ realizes the invariant difference $n-i$. For stars, the center is a universal reference point, so leaf labels equal edge differences.

These principles suggest broader constructions. A caterpillar could combine alternating labels along its non-leaf spine with allocated blocks for leaves. An olive tree could coordinate several pathwise sequences sharing a common extreme label at the root.

The counting theorem operates at a complementary level. Gracefulness organizes one graph copy internally; partition counting organizes many copies externally. Distinct differences may encode edge orbits under translation, but only disjointness and coverage turn that encoding into a decomposition.

The limitations are explicit. The results do not prove that every caterpillar is graceful, do not provide a general olive-tree construction, and do not settle the Graceful Tree Conjecture. Divisibility also does not imply decomposability. These boundaries identify the obligations for future work.

\section{Future work}

A first target is the full caterpillar theorem: define a caterpillar as a tree whose non-leaf vertices induce a path, then construct all differences from $1$ to the number of edges. A second is an explicit formula for olive trees obtained by identifying one endpoint of paths of lengths $1,2,\ldots,k$. Bounded exhaustive search can test formulas before general proofs are attempted.

A local problem concerns leaf extension. Starting from a graceful $m$-edge tree, attach a leaf and seek a graceful labeling with parameter $m+1$. The new edge must realize the new largest difference, suggesting attachment near labels $0$ or $m$, but a precise criterion remains to be developed.

A global direction is cyclic complete-graph decomposition. Given a graceful tree with $m$ edges, place its labels in a cyclic group of order $2m+1$ and translate the copy. The decisive tasks are to prove that translated undirected edge sets are disjoint and cover the complete graph. The Edge-Partition Counting Theorem then gives the cardinality identity.

Finally, bounded-spine caterpillars invite parameterized algorithms. For each fixed number of spine vertices, one may seek a recursive labeling procedure depending on leaf multiplicities. Such an algorithm would be constructive and experimentally falsifiable on bounded inputs.

\section{Conclusion}

Every finite path and every finite star admits an explicit graceful labeling. For the $n$-edge path, the alternating formula $0,n,1,n-1,2,n-2,\ldots$ produces differences $n,n-1,\ldots,1$. For the $n$-edge star, assigning $0$ to the center and $1,\ldots,n$ to the leaves produces radial differences $1,\ldots,n$. Both constructions are linear-time and supply immediate certificates.

If a finite host graph is exactly partitioned into $r$ pieces of $m$ edges, it has $rm$ edges. This principle gives the necessary divisibility condition for decompositions into equal graph copies and clarifies the arithmetic role of graceful difference spectra.

Together, these results isolate three durable ideas: alternate between extremes along a linear structure, anchor branching at an extreme label, and count disjoint pieces only after exact coverage is established. They solve two basic infinite families and provide a platform for broader graceful-tree and graph-decomposition constructions.

