# Graceful Trees: An Interactive Journey

A graceful labeling gives distinct numbers from $0$ to $m$ to the vertices of an $m$-edge graph and makes the edge differences exactly $1,2,\ldots,m$. Begin with the visual intuition in [graph labeling](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_labeling), then experiment below.

## 1. Alternate the extremes

A path becomes graceful when its labels read $0,n,1,n-1,\ldots$. Move the slider and watch the differences descend.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the path proof</summary>
At position $i$, use $i/2$ when $i$ is even and $n-\lfloor i/2floor$ when $i$ is odd. The even positions rise from zero and the odd positions fall from $n$, without collision. A two-case parity calculation gives $|L(i+1)-L(i)|=n-i$, so the differences are $n,n-1,\ldots,1$.
</details>

## 2. Count radially from zero

A star uses the other basic mechanism: anchor its center at $0$ and label its leaves $1$ through $n$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the star proof</summary>
Every leaf label is distinct and belongs to the permitted interval. The edge from the center to leaf $j$ has difference $|j-0|=j$, so all required differences occur exactly once.
</details>

## 3. From labels to decompositions

An exact edge partition covers every host edge once. If there are $r$ equally sized pieces and each has $m$ edges, disjoint-union counting gives $|E|=rm$. See [graph decomposition](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_decomposition) for broader context.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the counting argument and its limitation</summary>
Exact coverage identifies the host edge set with the union of the pieces. Unique membership makes that union disjoint, so cardinalities add. The identity is necessary for a decomposition, but not sufficient: divisibility alone does not prove coverage or prevent overlap.
</details>

## Where next?

Paths and stars are proved infinite families; the universal Graceful Tree Conjecture remains open. Promising targets include caterpillars, olive trees, extremal leaf extensions, and cyclic translations in [cyclic groups](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclic_group). Each proposed construction should be tested for repeated labels, missing differences, overlap, and uncovered edges before a general proof is sought.

